course: dictionary
lesson: 4519
title: Headlands and the sea
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A headland is a high piece of land with steep cliffs that sticks out into the sea. The word gives a clue: the head is the front of the land, so a headland is land that pushes its head out into the water.
explain: The sentences give more clues. «Waves cut caves into headlands, the caves join into arches, and when arches fall, stacks are left.» The waves attack the headlands, so headlands must be solid land at the edge of the sea.
explain: Headlands and bays happen side by side. «Where hard and soft rock lie side by side, the soft rock wears back into bays and the hard rock sticks out as headlands.» Hard rock wears away slowly, so it stays sticking out, while soft rock is eaten away into a bay.
fact: A headland is a high piece of land with steep cliffs that sticks out into the sea.
fact: Headlands form where hard rock wears away slowly.
fact: Soft rock next to a headland wears back faster and forms a bay.
fact: Waves attack headlands and make caves, arches and stacks.
fact: In stories, lighthouses often stand on headlands.
exercise: «Waves cut caves into headlands.» What must a headland be for waves to cut caves into it? | A boat floating on the water | *Solid land at the edge of the sea | A flat field far from the sea | A river flowing into a lake
exercise: «Where hard and soft rock lie side by side, the soft rock wears back into bays.» What is left sticking out into the sea? | Sand dunes | Bays | Rivers | *Headlands
exercise: Which rock wears away slowly and is left sticking out to form a headland? | Water | Soft rock | *Hard rock | Sand
test: What is a headland? | A flat field by a river | *A high piece of land with steep cliffs that sticks out into the sea | A deep lake in the hills | A small sandy beach
test: Where are lighthouses often built in stories? | Inside caves | Under the sea | In the middle of a city | *On headlands
exam: Which of these do waves make in headlands? | Forests | *Caves | Fields | Lakes
